  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "R6 and R12 directly show that adding Cr to a closely related refractory BCC alloy promotes Cr-Ta Laves-phase formation and increases hardness through precipitation and solid-solution strengthening. R2 and R19 further establish Laves precipitation and lattice-strain solid-solution hardening in similar Cr-containing refractory alloys. R14-R16 show more generally that controlling the topology of a hard second phase within a continuous ductile matrix can improve the strength-ductility balance. However, none of the records discloses or predicts a reticulated multicomponent Laves network that hinders crack propagation and compensates plasticity; indeed, R8 treats C14 Laves formation as detrimental because it promotes cracking. Thus the records strongly motivate Cr alloying and its strengthening effects but do not make the later paper's crucial network-mediated ductility mechanism readily reconstructible."
